{
  "dom_id": "step-q90713",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
